Restore the audio.cpp fork's im2col activation type in the conv graph builders
(tagged MINITTS_CONV_IM2COL_WEIGHT_TYPE in this tree; the fork itself left this
delta UNMARKED, which is why the MINITTS_* marker sweep missed it).

Upstream 0.20.2's ggml_conv_1d / ggml_conv_1d_dw / ggml_conv_2d lower to
im2col with dst type `a->type == BF16 ? F32 : F16`, so F32 weights get their
im2col'd activations rounded to F16 before the matmul. The fork lowers to
`a->type`: F32 weights mean an F32 conv end to end. Every framework audio
model loads conv weights as F32 (backend_weight_store load_f32_tensor), so
the convergence silently demoted every framework convolution to F16
activations.

Found numerically, not by inspection: flashsr_utility_test checks the FlashSR
decoder against onnxruntime-generated fixtures with a 2e-4 max-abs bound, and
after the convergence it failed at 1.7e-3 max / 3.3e-4 mean -- identically on
CPU and CUDA, which is the signature of a graph-builder (backend-independent)
delta rather than a kernel bug. The same binary against the fork tree passes.
Restoring `a->type` returns it to green with no other test moving.

ggml_conv_2d_dw is deliberately NOT touched: the fork kept upstream's
hardcoded F16 there, so upstream 0.20.2 (F16-unless-BF16) is already a
superset of the fork's behaviour for that builder.

